[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="CombatDef" load_steps=2 format=3]

; The Barred Gate's fighter, and the shape of its difficulty.
;
; The curve is two steps and stops: this is a quest of about twenty minutes, and a level cap
; the data states is better than one the player discovers by grinding into a wall.
;
; [20, 24] puts level 2 exactly at the hollow's two required fights (a pair of slinks apiece,
; 10 + 10) and level 3 exactly at the Keeper. That is not a coincidence to be tidied away - it
; is the whole difficulty design, and every number below was chosen against it. See
; docs/DECISIONS.md for the arithmetic.
;
; It was [10, 12] until M29 made every fight a formation. The awards were left alone and the
; CURVE doubled, which reproduces every beat exactly: the first hollow fight is still short of a
; level, the second still lands on 2, the optional cave pair is still worth precisely one level
; (24 xp against a 24-step), and the Keeper is still met at 2 and left at 3. Halving each
; enemy's xp instead would have needed awards of two and a half.
;
; The cues are frames, not seconds. These are the SECOND set of values: the first shipped
; without anyone playing the game, and a play-test killed them in one sentence - "way too fast
; for human reaction". They were: cues of 36 and 48, and a window of 8.
;
; The mechanism behind that, worth stating because it is the thing to size against: the `!`
; lights exactly WHEN the window opens (BattleLogic.cue_on is the same comparison the press is
; judged by, deliberately - the screen and the rule must be one fact). So the window is not a
; margin around a beat the player already saw coming; it IS the whole reaction budget. Eight
; frames is 133ms, and a person needs about 250ms to see a thing and move, before the display
; and the browser have their share.
;
; So: 24 frames, 400ms, is the reaction. The cues grew with it - 72 and 84 - because the window
; has to stay a small fraction of the wind-up, or pressing the moment the swing starts happens
; to land inside it and "timing" collapses back into "press early". The enemy's is still the
; longer of the two: somebody else's move needs an extra beat to read as one.
;
; None of this touches balance. The window is not in the damage arithmetic - a timed hit is
; worth double whenever it lands - so the fight the numbers were tuned against is the same
; fight; test_battle_content.gd proves that on every run.
;
; The magic curve is 8 and 3, which is deliberately small: at level 1 that is two casts of a
; three-point spell, and by level 3 it is four. Magic in a twenty-minute quest is a thing you
; decide to spend, not a second attack button - and an inn refills it, which is what gives the
; four gold a night a second reason to exist. UNJUDGED BY EAR: the gates spend the purse every
; run and cannot say whether the price bites.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://scripts/data/combat_def.gd" id="1_combat"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_combat")
id = &"quest_combat"
base_hp = 20
hp_per_level = 4
base_attack = 5
attack_per_level = 2
base_defense = 1
defense_per_level = 1
base_mp = 8
mp_per_level = 3
xp_curve = Array[int]([20, 24])
attack_cue_frames = 72
defend_cue_frames = 84
timed_window_frames = 24
message_frames = 45
